#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:73
msgid ""
"The kernel is the core of Linux. A kernel update does not replace the old "
"kernel — it installs a complete new one next to it, so you can still start "
"the previous version from the boot menu if the new one causes problems."
msgstr ""

#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:74
msgid ""
"Every kernel update adds roughly 300–500 MB (the kernel, its drivers and "
"headers). Old ones are only removed when someone runs “apt autoremove”, so "
"they pile up. On computers with a separate /boot partition they can even "
"fill it up and make updates fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:75
msgid ""
"Yes, because of two rules this app always follows: it never touches the "
"kernel you are running right now, and it always keeps at least two kernels — "
"the newest one plus one fallback. Only versions older than those are offered."
msgstr ""

#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:76
msgid ""
"Removes all packages of the selected kernel versions with “apt-get purge”. "
"First it asks APT for a dry run, and if APT would remove anything else than "
"what you see here, it stops."
msgstr ""

#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:77
msgid ""
"After a kernel update, restart your computer before cleaning so the new "
"kernel is the one in use."
msgstr ""

#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:82 src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:1030
msgid "Old kernels"
msgstr ""

#: src/safe_disk_cleaner/scanners.py:86
msgid ""
"Only needed on Debian/Ubuntu-based systems. Fedora keeps just the 3 newest "
"kernels automatically, and Arch keeps only one."
